  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )+12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +22 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-7A+22I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-7A+22I) & =O \\\\ A-7I+22{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{22}I-\\frac{1}{22}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{7}{22},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{22},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{7}{22}+(-\\frac{1}{22})=\\frac{3}{11}.\n$$",
